<!-- Deep Dive: Technical Advantages -->
<section class="section" aria-label="Technical Deep Dives">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <div class="section-label-pill">Under the Hood</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Two tuned models, two different disclosures</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Both apps solved the same problem: a general-purpose transcript needs cleanup a raw ASR model cannot do on its own. The difference is what each project tells you about how.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="deep-dive-grid">
      <div class="deep-dive-card reveal">
        <div class="deep-dive-icon">🔀</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-title">Two engines to switch between, or five to choose from</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-body">
          <p>FluidVoice gives you a real menu: Nemotron, Parakeet, Cohere, Whisper, or Apple Speech, each with its own language coverage and speed profile. If you want to hand-tune which engine handles which language, that flexibility is real and it is one of the clearest reasons to pick FluidVoice.</p>
          <p>EnviousWispr keeps it to two: Parakeet TDT v3 for the 25 languages it covers best, and WhisperKit filling in the other 74, switched manually under Settings, Transcription. There is no larger menu; the app is built around this one pairing.</p>
          <div class="deep-dive-detail">
            <strong>Why it matters:</strong> Power users who want to compare engines directly, or who need a specific one of FluidVoice's five, get more choice there today. Anyone who wants one setup that already works gets that from EnviousWispr.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="deep-dive-card reveal">
        <div class="deep-dive-icon">📋</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-title">Same idea, different paper trail</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-body">
          <p>Fluid-1 is a genuine achievement: a model fine-tuned specifically for turning rough dictation into clean text, shipped free with the app, no prompt required. EG-1 exists for the same reason and works the same way. Neither app makes you write your own cleanup instructions.</p>
          <p>Where they differ is disclosure. Our README names EG-1's base model, states its license terms, and explains what you can and cannot do with it. FluidVoice's app code is GPLv3, but the Fluid-1 runtime and weights sit outside that repository, and we could not find a published license or model card for it as of this writing. That may simply mean it has not been written yet, not that it does not exist; we welcome a correction if one is published.</p>
          <div class="deep-dive-detail">
            <strong>Why it matters:</strong> If you care about knowing exactly what model is processing your words and under what terms, that information is public for EG-1 today. For Fluid-1, it was not something we could find.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

